# RS3 plugin.json manifest

`plugin.json` is the only metadata the client and moderators read. The fields are documented at https://developer.runescape.com/rs/structure.html#manifest and enforced by `packages/schema/plugin.schema.json`.

## Applies to

- `plugin.json` at the root of every plugin folder.

## Rules

- Always present: `name`, `version`, `contactEmailAddress`. The email is a legal requirement; use one that is not your mod.io login.
- `requiredDependencies` and `optionalDependencies` are either arrays of plugin names or maps of Lua alias to plugin name. Do not mix forms inside one field.
- `requiredPermissions` and `optionalPermissions` contain only `httpAccess`, `chatAccess`, `trackingAccess`. A permission appears in one list, never both.
- `urls` maps Lua identifiers to `http://` or `https://` base URLs and is only meaningful with `httpAccess`.
- `hasSettingsIcon` and `hasRibbonIcon` opt into `Event.SettingsLayerReady` and `Event.ContentLayerReady`.
- Do not hand-write `remote*` fields; the plugin repository sets them.
- Add the `$schema` line so the editor validates while you type, and run `rs3dev validate` before syncing.

## Examples

### Good

```json
{
  "$schema": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/TMHSDigital/RS3-Developer-Tools/main/packages/schema/plugin.schema.json",
  "name": "Fish Log",
  "version": "0.3.0",
  "contactEmailAddress": "fishlog-support@example.com",
  "requiredDependencies": [],
  "optionalDependencies": { "Pretty": "PrettyUI" },
  "requiredPermissions": [],
  "optionalPermissions": ["chatAccess"]
}
```

### Bad

```json
{ "name": "Fish Log", "version": "0.3.0", "permissions": ["chat"] }
```
